{
  "title": "Symmetric Tree",
  "description": "Decide whether a binary tree equals its own mirror image.",
  "statement": "A binary tree is **symmetric** when its left and right subtrees are mirror images of each other. Formally, the tree is symmetric when its root is trivially in place and, for every node, the left subtree of one node is the mirror of the right subtree of its mirror partner - that is, values must be equal and shapes must be identical after swapping left and right.\n\nTwo trees can share the same values yet differ in shape, and then they are not mirror images: shape counts as much as content.\n\nCompare two subtrees at a time, swapping the child roles on every step, and report the verdict. A tree with a single node is symmetric.",
  "inputFormat": "The input is read from standard input as exactly one line:\n\n1. Line 1: the tree in level-order (breadth-first) serialisation. Values are separated by commas with **no spaces**, and the literal `null` marks a missing child. The line always starts with the root value and the tree always has at least one node (up to `10^5` nodes).",
  "outputFormat": "Print a single line containing the lowercase word `true` if the tree is symmetric, otherwise the lowercase word `false`.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= number of nodes <= 10^5",
    "-10^4 <= node value <= 10^4",
    "The level-order line contains no spaces around the commas",
    "Trailing `null` entries are not used; the line stops once the last real node is written"
  ],
  "notes": "Do not stop at the first missing child of a subtree: a `null` on one side must be matched against a `null` on the other side. Comparing only values (or only shapes) gives the wrong answer on trees such as `1,2,2,null,null,3,3`, where values match but the two halves have different depths. The 1023-node boundary case forces an iterative approach in languages with a small call stack.",
  "difficulty": "EASY",
  "tags": [
    "tree",
    "recursion",
    "queue"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 1000,
  "memoryLimitKb": 256000,
  "starterCode": {
    "python": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    levelOrder = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    # TODO: print the result\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n",
    "javascript": "const fs = require('fs');\nconst levelOrder = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').trim();\nconst tokens = levelOrder.length === 0 ? [] : levelOrder.split(',');\n// TODO: print the result\n",
    "java": "import java.io.BufferedReader;\nimport java.io.InputStreamReader;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String line = br.readLine();\n        List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (line != null && !line.isEmpty()) {\n            for (String part : line.split(\",\")) {\n                tokens.add(part.trim());\n            }\n        }\n        // TODO: print the result\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    string line;\n    getline(cin, line);\n    vector<string> tokens;\n    stringstream ss(line);\n    string token;\n    while (getline(ss, token, ',')) {\n        tokens.push_back(token);\n    }\n\n    // TODO: print the result\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
